      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "It has been suggested that obese children have lower cognitive function, demonstrate poorer physical performance and are more susceptible to social-emotional problems."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "AIMS",
          "content": "To describe associations between human physical growth, cognitive development, physical fitness and social-emotional characteristics of obese and non-obese children and to verify the predictors of intellectual coefficient by socioeconomic status (SES)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "SUBJECTS AND METHODS",
          "content": "A sample of 107 non-obese (N-Ob) children [-1\u2009z-score body mass index (BMI) ≤1\u2009z-score] and 108 obese (Ob) children [2 z-score ≤BMI ≤5\u2009z-score] from a larger cohort was evaluated. Intellectual coefficient (IQ), social-emotional wellbeing (SEW), 6-minute walk test (6MWT) and SES (mid-low, low and very low) were assessed."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Ob children were taller, heavier and present more height for age and BMI than N-Ob children (p\u2009<\u20090.001). A significant correlation between IQ and SEW (r\u2009=\u20090.14), 6MWT and BMI z-score (r\u2009=\u2009-0.18) and 6MWT and SEW (r\u2009=\u20090.15) was found. Multiple regression analysis revealed that BMI z-score had a negative impact on IQ in the mid-low SES sub-group and that SEW had a positive effect on IQ in the very-low SES sub-group."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "In Chilean pre-school children from low-income families cognitive ability varied according to SES."
        }
      ]},
